    "body": "A new factory near Raleigh, North Carolina, will assemble and test electrical equipment before it ships to data centers and other project sites. Siemens said on Tuesday that it opened the 131,000-square-foot plant last week and expects it to create up to 200 skilled manufacturing jobs .\nWhat’s Related\nThe factory builds power skids, which combine pieces of electrical equipment on a single platform. Siemens assembles, connects, and tests the equipment at the plant before shipping it as a unit. That leaves less work for crews at the construction site and can help data center operators bring new capacity online sooner.\nPart of a $165 million-plus expansion\nSiemens first announced the Raleigh-area factory in March as part of a plan to invest more than $165 million in manufacturing across North and South Carolina. At the time, the company expected the Raleigh-area site to add 100 jobs by the end of 2026. Its opening announcement put the total at up to 200 jobs and gave no hiring timeline for the full tally.\nThe March plan also included expanding switchgear production in Wendell, North Carolina, and building a new lighting panel plant and distribution center in Spartanburg, South Carolina. Siemens said the investments would help it fill record orders for electrical equipment used in data centers.\nSiemens is working with Wake Technical Community College to train new hires. The college’s onboarding program, Operation Boot Camp, teaches basic mechanical and electrical skills used in manufacturing.",
